{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b3","Decomposition by the radical","<p><em>\nFor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>rad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>rad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The first equality\nfollows from the definition of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></math></span>; the second is ordinary reduction\nof an integer fraction. Multiplication by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> can cause further\ncancellation. No coprimality assumption is included in either\nidentity.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b4","A cyclotomic congruence","<p><em> Let\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> be squarefree and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>. In <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nwhere <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is the second Jordan totient. Evaluation at two\ngives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b8a","Adjoining a prime: new divisors","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span> be prime, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b8b","Adjoining a prime: existing\ndivisors","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> be\nsquarefree, let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span> be prime,\nand let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>r</mi></math></span>. For the existing\ndivisors <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>, the corresponding\nidentity is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d7","Lambert-series identities","<p><em> For\nan arithmetic function <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>f</mi></math></span> for which\nthe sums converge absolutely, write <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Expanding\neach geometric series gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nIn particular, if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>μ</mi></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></math></span>. The table distinguishes this\nvalue from four other Lambert-series values. The identity changes the\ncoefficients and the form of the denominator; it does not transfer an\nirrationality theorem for one weight to another.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e3","An affine combination annihilates\nthe specified divisor terms","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. The <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>th term in the Möbius expansion of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, where </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThis is affine in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>. Consequently,\nfor any finite family of real coefficients <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span> and nonnegative integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, one has\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>For the four multipliers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and coefficients <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe zeroth and first moments are zero, while the second is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>15</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>152</mn></math></span>.\nEquivalently, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> takes the\nvalues <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>152</mn></math></span> at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This explains the\ncoefficients: they cancel the affine contribution of each divisor of\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi></math></span>, without cancelling a general\nquadratic function of the multiplier.</em></p>\n<p><em>For a finite truncation of depth <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, write </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\ndifference between <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>U</mi></math></span> has absolute value at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>19</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>. Indeed, the error equals </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>15</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nand both parenthesised terms lie between <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>19</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span> by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Thus </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>19</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>19</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nis sufficient for this four-tail combination to be nonintegral. The\nbound is derived from these tail enclosures; no optimality for the\nactual totient tails is asserted. Obtaining such certificates at the\nrequired unbounded family of LCM heights remains unproved, as in\nTheorems <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:e2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:e2\">6.60</a>\nand <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b6\">6.52</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:f1","The error under composite\ndilation","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. If\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed,\npartition the divisors of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></math></span> into\nthose already dividing <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span>, the\npossible new divisor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>, and all\nother new divisors. If every element of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> is prime, the last set is empty, since\na prime dividing <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></math></span> but not <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> must equal the prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>. For composite <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span> it can be nonempty: at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, the other new divisors are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>This is an unweighted counting identity. The associated Lambert\nseries satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For the totient series the\nrequired divisor-convolution weight is instead <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>μ</mi></math></span>, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></math></span>, as in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:d7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d7\">6.43</a>. Weighting all\ndivisors by <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi></math></span> would give\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>, not\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>; already at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> these values are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. A weighted version must retain the\nweights of the new divisors, so it cannot be obtained by substituting\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> in this unweighted\nformula. Also <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> for\nprimes <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>, so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>α</mi></math></span> is unbounded and is not a periodic\nweight.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a10","The quantified certificate condition is\nequivalent to irrationality","<p><em> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">↔</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The quantified-condition side\nis exactly <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nquantified as <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi></math></span>. The universally\nquantified assertion remains unproved. The equivalence identifies the\nfinite witnesses that suffice; it does not establish their existence\nbeyond every threshold.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b2","It suffices to use multiples of a\nperiod","<p><em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The\nshift may be any positive multiple of a prescribed period. This enlarges\nthe choice of finite witness: Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a10\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a10\">6.47</a> gives the\ncondition with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Conversely,\nrationality would make all such tail differences integral after a fixed\nstarting index. The displayed implication therefore makes this\nquantified condition equivalent to irrationality; its truth remains\nunproved.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b3","The diagonal condition is equivalent to\nirrationality","<p><em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">↔</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. For a fixed\nhypothetical rational value, sufficiently large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> makes both its dyadic denominator and\nits odd-part period admissible at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Conversely, irrationality and\npointwise completeness supply a witness for every prescribed <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>. This is an equivalent condition with\none scale parameter; the verified cases <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>82</mn></math></span> do not establish it at arbitrarily\nlarge scales.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b4","Nondivisors in a short LCM window","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></math></span> be integers. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></math></span> for a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> and an integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Indeed, a prime-power divisor of\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> must exceed <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></math></span> leaves no room for a cofactor\nlarger than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. This classifies the\nexceptional offsets in this short window; it does not bound their\ncontribution to a weighted sum of totient differences.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b5","Totient factorisation on an LCM\nprogression","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be integers. Suppose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and every prime divisor of\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> also divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The two\nfactors in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> are\ncoprime: the second is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> modulo\neach prime dividing <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span>. Totient\nmultiplicativity gives the identity. The extra prime-divisor hypothesis\nis not automatic from <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>;\nfor example, it fails at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>,\nwhere <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> but the\nproposed product would be <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b6","Rationality forces tail integrality on\nan LCM grid","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that for all <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThus all tails at positive multiples of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> have the same fractional part once\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></span>. A certificate at one\nfixed scale does not refute rationality: the threshold <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></span> depends on the hypothetical rational\nvalue. At a shift <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and\nbasepoint <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, such a certificate\nexcludes rational values whose reduced denominator divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Certificates at\narbitrarily large scales, as required in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b7\">6.53</a>, exclude every\nhypothetical rational value.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b7","A sufficient nonintegrality condition\non the grid","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there are integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Rationality would make\nthe corresponding tail difference integral at every sufficiently large\nscale, contradicting Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a6\">6.18</a>. A successful\ncertificate necessarily has <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>,\nsince the discrepancy vanishes at shift zero.</em></p>\n<p><em>The diagonal choice is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>; consecutive grid points have <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> the pair <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The diagonal equivalence\nin Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b3\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b3\">6.49</a>\nalso gives the converse from irrationality to the displayed quantified\ncondition; that condition remains unproved.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b8","Equivalent conditions stated without\ncertificates","<p><em> The series <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> is\nirrational if and only if </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mtext>for arbitrarily large integers </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Equivalently,\nfor arbitrarily large <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> there are\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>.\nApply the pointwise equivalence of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a7\">6.19</a> to Theorems <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b3\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b3\">6.49</a> and <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b7\">6.53</a>, respectively.\nThese are equivalent statements of the irrationality question; the\nrequired unbounded sets of scales are not established.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b9a","Soundness of a second-difference\ncertificate","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>. If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The\nnumerator is the second difference of the finite windows. Subtracting\nthe two tail identities leaves an error of absolute value at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, which explains the radius in\nthe hypothesis. At <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, the\nfirst-difference test holds at depth <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>8</mn></math></span>, whereas the second-difference test\nfails at every depth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span> and\nholds at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math></span>. A historical probe\nover <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></math></span> reported that the\nfirst-difference test was at least as shallow in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>30</mn></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>40</mn></math></span> sampled cases. This finite comparison\nis not a uniform ordering of the depths.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=ba7ef333d530"}
